                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "After that ending, Danganronpa V3 is now my favourite in the series. I was kinda feeling that way as it went along but not only does its final trial retroactively improve and recontextualise much of the decisions made so far but it also stands tall as an incredibly poignant and emotional art piece. I feel like I finally 'get' Danganronpa. Not that I didn't 'get' the fun of the campy universe and eccentric characters established in THH or the wackier ideas of SDR2, but instead Danganronpa now has a strong thesis statement.\n\nBut enough of the waxing poetic, V3 was so fun to play. My friend group and I voiced the characters lines between trials and its never been more engaging to bring such a fantastic group of characters to life. This cast is the most consistently great of the lot including a few top tiers of the series and no complete stinkers like Teruteru.\n\nAlthough it is longest of the games, the pace of the story really does it a favour, especially as you get into the rhythm of it. There is however a bit of annoying repeated dialogue with many key points being reiterated, ie. I KNOW KOKICHI CAN PICK LOCKS DEAR GOD. Each trial always keeps you on your toes with several thrilling twists and subversions. Even in the first one as the rug is completely pulled out from under you. It maintains several big mysteries at once and builds on arcs and storylines throughout, none of the chapters feel like the story is spinning its wheels.\n\nIt's also very mechanically engaging, improving on the formula by having great trials and minigames within. [spoiler]There's even a really neat switch up with the Virtual World.[/spoiler]\n\nThere is one major flaw though and that is the Monokubs. While they are thematically pertinent, they are endlessly aggravating and make up about 15% of total dialogue and disrupted multiple important sequences with their bullshit. It is a detriment to the early game as a whole and can make it perhaps hard to get into. [spoiler]But you do get the satisfaction of seeing each of the little fuckers put down.[/spoiler]\n\nIn the end, it all comes down to that final trial. [spoiler]The game forces you into the most uncomfortable corner so you feel just like the survivors. You have to face the most painful despair and it knows exactly how you will react, pushing your discomfort in your face. You must reevaluate the ideas of hope you once thought so comforting. Understand the true horror behind being a fan of something like Danganronpa. But ultimately, you need to understand that all the memories and connections made are not for nothing, these characters, stories and experiences have affected you and saying goodbye, as hard as it may be, is not forgoing that which made it important. Fiction is reality because every part of it that happened made you react, talk, laugh, cry, get angry, theorise, draw and think.[/spoiler] Danganronpa will be with you forever and my favourite part of V3s message is that it knows that one day you will understand that.",
